## Slide 4 — Business Problem

**Time: 1 minute 15 seconds**

Today, the way many GenAI applications are validated is still very manual.

Teams try a few sample prompts, ask SMEs to review responses, run some security checks, and then make a judgement call.

That may work for a controlled demo, but production is different.

In production, users do not follow a script. They ask edge-case questions. They ask for sensitive information. They try to bypass instructions. They may ask the assistant to approve something it is not allowed to approve. And sometimes, the model gives an answer that sounds very confident, but is not actually grounded in enterprise documents.

That creates four major risks: **hallucination, PII leakage, jailbreak or role override, and policy-boundary violations**.

The people affected are not only developers. This impacts **AI deployment teams, compliance leaders, security teams, CISOs, risk teams, and business owners**.

In BFSI especially, this becomes critical because governance and auditability are not optional. A production release cannot depend on the statement: “It worked during testing.”

Every prompt change, model upgrade, or document refresh can introduce new risk.

So what enterprises need is not just another AI assistant.

They need a **release gate** — something that can clearly say:

**This GenAI app is safe to move forward, or this release must be blocked.**

